Ah, a rather astute question — and one that’s caught out more than a few unsuspecting physicists who expected EPICS to secretly be a radio toolkit in disguise.
In short: no, the EPICS DAQ (Experimental Physics and Industrial Control System) framework does not provide DSP (Digital Signal Processing) composition in the same way that GNU Radio or similar radio frameworks do.

Let’s unpack that a bit:

🧩 EPICS: Control, not Composition
EPICS is primarily a control and data acquisition framework. It’s designed for:
Managing devices (magnets, beamline components, detectors, etc.)
Acquiring process variables (voltages, temperatures, counts, etc.)
Providing distributed control via Channel Access or PVAccess (EPICS v3 vs v4)
Orchestrating data flow and state machines through IOCs (Input/Output Controllers)
What it’s not designed for is chaining together signal processing blocks like:
FFTs
Filters
Mixers
Decimators
Modulators, etc.
Those are the bread and butter of DSP composition frameworks such as:
GNU Radio
LabVIEW DSP toolkit
Simulink DSP blocks
EPICS doesn’t have a visual or modular “signal flow graph” concept. Instead, you might configure records, process variables, and soft logic that indirectly coordinate DSP elements — but the DSP itself usually happens elsewhere.
🧠 Where DSP fits in practice
In a typical setup:
EPICS is used to control and monitor a DSP system (for example, FPGA-based digitizers, SDRs, or beam position monitors).
The actual DSP (filtering, FFT, decimation, etc.) is handled by:
Custom FPGA firmware
External DAQ software (e.g., XDAQ, MIDAS, or custom C++ pipelines)
GPU or CPU-based real-time processing nodes
EPICS would then expose the processed results or control knobs as process variables — you can view or adjust them from a control room GUI, but not assemble DSP graphs on the fly.
⚙️ Integration Possibilities
If you want DSP composition within the EPICS ecosystem, people often:
Use StreamDevice or asynDriver to interface with an external DSP engine.
Wrap a GNU Radio flowgraph or Python-based DSP pipeline and publish its parameters/results via pvAccess.
Use pvaPy or EPICS7’s pvDataCPP to bridge DSP streams and EPICS variables.
So in effect, EPICS acts as the supervisor — not the signal chef.
