ChatGPT said:
Excellent — you’ve now stepped into one of the more interesting corners of EPICS: areaDetector, the framework that looks like it’s just for cameras but can, in fact, be turned into a quite capable signal processing pipeline, provided one knows how to twist its arm gently.
Let’s unpack this properly, with a little British-style understatement: areaDetector wasn’t designed for DSP per se — but it’s remarkably good at it when persuaded politely.

🎥 What areaDetector is (at heart)
At its core, areaDetector is a modular EPICS framework for handling array data, most often 2D images from scientific cameras, detectors, or sensors.
However, the key insight is that it doesn’t actually care what’s inside those arrays — photons, voltages, FFT bins, tea leaf densities — it’s all just structured numeric data to it.
The framework provides:

A standard plugin architecture for data processing and routing.
A data flow model, where NDArray objects (multi-dimensional arrays with metadata) move from one plugin to another.
Support for both hardware drivers and software filters.
In essence: areaDetector is a stream processing pipeline, just not marketed as such.
🧠 Reinterpreting areaDetector as a Signal Processing Engine
Let’s imagine you want to process a signal — say a waveform, or a stream of digitised data.
You could easily represent this as a 1D or 2D NDArray, and use areaDetector’s existing plugins for surprisingly DSP-like functionality.
🧩 Typical processing chain
Here’s a canonical example:
Stage	Plugin	Function
Input	NDPluginDriver or custom driver	Acquire raw waveform data (e.g. from an ADC, FPGA, or simulated source)
Filtering	NDPluginProcess	Perform arithmetic, offsets, scaling, averaging, or basic filtering
Transform	NDPluginFFT	Compute FFTs or power spectra
Analysis	NDPluginStats	Extract mean, variance, centroid, etc.
Thresholding	NDPluginROI or NDPluginOverlay	Define regions of interest or masks (useful in frequency domain)
Output	NDPluginStdArrays or NDPluginFile	Stream processed results via EPICS PVs or save to disk
Visualisation	ADViewers (ImageJ, CSS BOY, etc.)	View live spectra or time series
⚙️ How you’d adapt it for general DSP
1. Use NDArray as your “signal packet”
Treat each NDArray as a frame of samples — e.g. 1×N arrays for time-domain data, or 2×N for complex IQ pairs.
2. Write a custom driver
You can subclass ADDriver to pull data from:
A digitiser or SDR card.
An FPGA stream (e.g. via PCIe or Ethernet).
A simulated or software DSP generator (Python, C++, etc.).
Your driver pushes NDArrays into the plugin chain.
3. Use or extend existing plugins
areaDetector already ships with NDPluginFFT, NDPluginProcess, NDPluginStats, and others — which can be composed in a surprisingly modular way.
For example:
Raw waveform → NDPluginProcess (DC removal) → NDPluginFFT → NDPluginStats
4. Add custom DSP plugins
If you need custom filtering, correlation, or phase detection, you can subclass NDPluginDriver to create your own plugin that operates on NDArrays in C++ or Python (via pvapy).
These can be daisy-chained dynamically, just like in GNU Radio.
5. Expose EPICS PVs for tuning
Every plugin parameter (gain, window size, ROI limits, FFT bins, etc.) appears as an EPICS process variable — meaning you can control the DSP pipeline live from a GUI or script.
🧮 Example: Turning areaDetector into a spectrum analyser
Imagine you’ve got a 1 MS/s digitiser.
Your custom ADDriver reads 1,024-sample frames into NDArrays.
Each NDArray passes into NDPluginFFT, producing amplitude spectra.
The spectra go through NDPluginStats to extract peak frequency.
NDPluginOverlay highlights dominant peaks visually.
The result is displayed in a viewer, and numerical results are available as PVs.
Voilà — you’ve built a reconfigurable spectrum analyser inside EPICS.
🪄 Why this approach is powerful
You get zero-copy data flow between plugins.
The metadata (NDAttributes) travel with the data, keeping time stamps and acquisition context intact.
You can reconfigure pipelines at runtime — add/remove plugins without stopping acquisition.
It integrates natively with EPICS PVs, GUIs, and archivers.
🧩 Caveats
areaDetector isn’t real-time DSP — it’s soft real-time; latencies depend on plugin scheduling.
Throughput is fine for MHz-range data, but not GHz-class radio streams.
Complex DSP (I/Q modulation, filtering, correlation) often needs custom plugin code or GPU acceleration (possible via CUDA-enabled plugins).
