So while people build “pipelines” in pvaPy-based streaming frameworks, they do it in code — not via a generic visual composer.
⚠️ Challenges in providing a visual composer for pvaPy pipelines
Before discussing how one could build something, it’s worth noting the challenges:
Dynamic structure: pvaPy pipelines may not have a rigid graph structure in advance; you might dynamically subscribe or spawn processing paths.
Heterogeneous operations: some stages are pure Python (NumPy / SciPy), others may call out to compiled libraries, some may be I/O-heavy, etc. A visual tool would need to wrap and parameterize arbitrary Python functions or modules.
State & side effects: Some pipeline nodes will have side effects or state (e.g. filters with memory, buffers, accumulations), which complicates a purely functional “node chain” model.
Latency, threading, scheduling: The execution order, concurrency, and timing all matter. A visual composer would have to expose or hide scheduling semantics.
EPICS integration: The nodes are not isolated — they interact with PVs, metadata, network delays, etc. A visual tool must bridge between the dataflow graph and EPICS’ channel model.
🛠 How one could build a visual composer for pvaPy pipelines
If you wanted to build or prototype a visual composer, here’s one possible architecture and sketch of how that might look:
1. Represent pipeline as a graph of nodes and edges.
Each node is a processing block (subscribe, filter, FFT, threshold, publish).
Edges represent the flow of NTNDArray or PV data between nodes.
2. Associate each node with a Python function or class
The visual tool generates Python “wrapper code” under the hood (or uses a reflection/injection mechanism).
Node parameters map to PV names, buffer sizes, algorithm parameters.
3. Use a GUI framework to allow drag/drop and wiring
For instance, in Qt (PyQt5 / PySide2), using QGraphicsScene you can place blocks and draw wires.
Or leverage web-based libraries: Node-RED, Draw2D, D3.js, etc.
Alternatively, Jupyter-based visual frameworks (ipywidgets + ipycanvas, dash, or bqplot) could host a simple compositor.
4. Backend: instantiate the graph in pvaPy
Once a user “deploys” the pipeline, the composer translates the visual graph into Python code or objects instantiating:
Channel subscribers for input nodes,
Processing loops (e.g. monitor() callbacks),
Channel publishers for output nodes.
Wire the node outputs to function inputs accordingly.
5. Live editing / hot-swapping
Allow nodes to be added/removed on the fly.
Provide live parameter editing (sliders, PV monitors).
Visual feedback (e.g. show data rates, latencies, health signals).
6. Monitoring & visualization
For each node, provide a small embedded plot (spectrum, time series) for debugging.
Use color coding or indicators for errors, throughput, backpressure.
🧪 A minimal “toy” prototype idea
Here’s how you might prototype a mini visual composer in Python + Jupyter:
